# PROLOGUE — "Soundcheck", part 1: the Pink Wyvern.
# Canon: docs/STORY.md (Prologue). Tone: PG-13, funny first, true second.
# Objectives are derived from flags (src/content/objectives.ts), so the player can do things in any order.
# Repeat talks never repeat: every talk scene branches on seen.<scene id> (times played before).

@scene prologue.intro
# PLOT: Open the game on Proclamation Night; set up the first chores.
# CHARACTER: Eddar is smart, funny, overlooked, and mostly at peace with it. The Ledger device starts, and he writes beautifully.
@bgm wyvern_soundcheck
@card SOLACE BAY — PROCLAMATION NIGHT
NARRATOR: Tonight the Rubric names the Hero of Legend. Every bar in Solace Bay is open late.
NARRATOR: Every bar needs someone to mop it.
@cam focus eddar
EDDAR: Three cola puddles, one mop, forty minutes to doors.
EDDAR: And somewhere, a prophecy deciding whose life matters. Spoiler: not the guy holding the mop.
LEDGER: Entry #2,204: Mopped the stage at six. At five past, the band, in their infinite and terrible wisdom, baptised it anew in cola. Mopped it at ten past. Was then asked, in a tone of genuine wonder, why the stage was wet. O tempora, o mores, o Spike.
@flag p.intro
@end

@scene prologue.puddle_1
# PLOT: Chore 1/3. CHARACTER: observational wit.
@sfx mop
EDDAR: Cola, lime, and something that glows. Spike's been mixing "tour drinks" again.
@flag p.puddle_1
@set puddles += 1
@if puddles>=3
EDDAR: Done. The floor is clean, the band is dirty, the universe is balanced.
@endif
@end

@scene prologue.puddle_2
# PLOT: Chore 2/3. CHARACTER: a little petty, a lot human.
@sfx mop
EDDAR: This one's shaped like Rex's face. I'll take my time.
EDDAR: ...Okay, that was cathartic.
@flag p.puddle_2
@set puddles += 1
@if puddles>=3
EDDAR: Done. The floor is clean, the band is dirty, the universe is balanced.
@endif
@end

@scene prologue.puddle_3
# PLOT: Chore 3/3. CHARACTER: the band loves him in their careless way.
@sfx mop
EDDAR: Somebody tracked roller-skate wax through the cola. On purpose. Into a spiral.
EDDAR: That's not a spill. That's a signature.
LULU: Love you, Eddar!
@flag p.puddle_3
@set puddles += 1
@if puddles>=3
EDDAR: Done. The floor is clean, the band is dirty, the universe is balanced.
@endif
@end

@scene prologue.roxy
# PLOT: Introduce Roxy and the amp problem; react to each stage of the fix.
# CHARACTER: her fuse, her ambition, her family; Eddar's crush; "lucky charm".
@if p.amp_fixed
@if talk.roxy_after>=1
ROXY [smile]: Stop hovering, lucky charm. You'll jinx her. Go do lucky-charm things somewhere else.
@end
@endif
ROXY [smile]: Listen to that. Clean as a church bell. You're a wizard, Eddar.
EDDAR: I'm a guy who reads amp manuals for fun.
ROXY: Same thing in this town.
@set talk.roxy_after += 1
@end
@endif
@if p.amp_hum_back
ROXY [angry]: It's BACK, Eddar. Three bars. I got three bars of glory and then BZZZ, like the universe clearing its throat at me.
EDDAR: Ground loops are like that. They hide.
ROXY [angry]: Then FIND it. Velvet Kane is going to walk in here in half an hour and I am not losing Amplitude to a toaster with a grudge.
@emote roxy fire
@end
@endif
@if p.amp_checked
@if seen.prologue.roxy>=2
ROXY [angry]: Eddar. Amp. Eddar. Amp. I'm drawing you a map with my eyes.
@end
@endif
ROXY [angry]: Well? Is she dying? Tell me she's not dying. I can't afford a new one. I can barely afford this ponytail.
EDDAR: Give me a second with it.
@end
@endif
@cam focus roxy
@sfx amp_buzz
ROXY [angry]: Do you hear that? That BZZZ? That's the sound of my career getting electrocuted.
EDDAR: It's a sixty-cycle hum. It's probably not personal.
ROXY [angry]: Everything is personal, Eddar. There's a booker from Amplitude in the crowd tonight. AMPLITUDE. If this thing buzzes through my solo, I'm going to set it on fire. And then the stage. And then myself.
@emote roxy fire
SPIKE: Her hair's doing the thing again.
ROXY [angry]: My hair is FINE.
? Offer to look at the amp. -> look
? Tell her the hair is a fire hazard. -> hazard {bond_roxy:-1}
? Ask about the booker. -> booker {bond_roxy:+1}
* booker
ROXY [smile]: Lady named Velvet Kane. Books the Grand Stadium. One good set and the Wildfires play Amplitude.
ROXY: Which means real money. Which means my brothers get shoes that fit before the next growth spurt.
EDDAR: Then the amp doesn't buzz tonight.
@goto look
* hazard
EDDAR: Respectfully, your hair is a fire hazard.
ROXY [angry]: Respectfully, so is your mouth. FIX. THE. AMP.
@goto look
* look
EDDAR: Let me look at it. Two minutes.
ROXY [smile]: You're my lucky charm, you know that?
LEDGER: Entry #2,205: "Lucky charm." Not sweetheart, not beloved, not even "date." A charm: a small dead rabbit's foot one keeps in a pocket and forgets until the dice are rolling. Still. She keeps it in her pocket.
@flag p.amp_checked
@end

@scene prologue.amp
# PLOT: Two-stage amp puzzle. The ground-lift fix is only a bandage; the real fault is the transformer,
# and Eddar fixes it by punching it in frustration. A violet flare nobody notices (plants his power, T4).
# CHARACTER: competence, then a rare loss of temper; the band's affection; Roxy's forehead kiss.
@if !p.amp_checked
EDDAR: Roxy's amp. "Property of Roxy Vega. Touch it and die." Fair enough. I'll ask first.
@end
@endif
@if p.amp_fixed
@if seen.prologue.amp>=3
EDDAR: There's a dent shaped like my knuckles in the casing. Roxy hasn't noticed. Roxy will notice.
@end
@endif
EDDAR: Purring like a cat that's eaten a smaller, louder cat.
@end
@endif
@if p.amp_hum_back
@goto round_two
@endif
@sfx amp_buzz
EDDAR: Tubes glowing. Cables seated. The buzz is coming from... everywhere and nowhere.
NARRATOR: Eddar closes his eyes. Under the buzz there's a second sound, faint and low, like the room itself humming.
EDDAR: There. The stage lights and the amp are fighting over the same ground wire.
* first_try
? Flip the ground-lift switch. -> lift {harmony:+1}
? Replace the power tube. -> tube
* tube
@sfx clunk
EDDAR: New tube. Same buzz. Two tokens' worth of glass, and the universe laughs at me.
@set tokens -= 2
EDDAR: Okay. Think. It's not the tube.
@goto first_try
* lift
@sfx switch
NARRATOR: Click. The buzz drops away.
ROXY [smile]: Oh — OH. Okay. Okay! Count it in, Lulu!
@sfx guitar_riff
NARRATOR: Roxy tears into the opening riff. One bar. Two. The neon over the stage brightens the way it does when someone in Solace Bay plays like they mean it. Three bars—
@sfx amp_buzz
NARRATOR: —and the hum comes back, louder, like it went out for reinforcements.
ROXY [angry]: EDDAR!
@emote roxy fire
SPIKE: Hair.
ROXY [angry]: I KNOW ABOUT THE HAIR, SPIKE.
LEDGER: Entry #2,206: Victory endured for three bars, which is longer than most of mine. The hum returned like a creditor. Roxy's hair returned to its natural state: on fire.
@flag p.amp_hum_back
@end
* round_two
@sfx amp_buzz
EDDAR: Okay. The ground was a bandage. The hum's coming from inside the chassis: the power transformer's singing.
? Swap the fuse. -> fuse
? Tighten the transformer mounts. -> mounts
* fuse
@sfx clunk
EDDAR: New fuse. Same song. Of course. Fuses never sing; they only die.
@goto temper
* mounts
@sfx clunk
EDDAR: Tight. Tighter. Tightest. Still humming. It's not loose, it's just rude.
@goto temper
* temper
NARRATOR: Thirty minutes to doors. A booker from Amplitude. Roxy's brothers' shoes. And one smug little transformer, humming as if none of it matters.
EDDAR: You know what? No.
? Hit it. -> punch
? Hit it, but politely. -> punch {harmony:+1}
* punch
@sfx punch
@fx violet eddar
@cam shake
NARRATOR: Eddar punches Roxy's amp.
NARRATOR: For half a heartbeat, the dent his knuckles leave glows violet. Then it's just a dent.
NARRATOR: The hum doesn't fade. It stops, the way a song stops when someone lifts the needle.
NARRATOR: Nobody notices the light. Not Spike, not Lulu, not even Eddar, who is busy shaking out his hand and swearing very quietly in the language of the deeply embarrassed.
SPIKE: Did you just punch Roxy's amp?
EDDAR: It was a diagnostic tap.
SPIKE: It worked.
EDDAR: Most diagnostics do, if you mean them.
@flag p.amp_fixed
@unflag p.amp_hum_back
@cam focus roxy
ROXY [smile]: Is that — oh, that's CLEAN. Listen to her!
@sfx guitar_riff
ROXY [smile]: C'mere, lucky charm.
@emote roxy kiss
NARRATOR: She kisses him on the forehead.
LEDGER: Entry #2,207: Forehead. Again. The brow is a fine and sacred country, I am told; but I should dearly like, one day, to visit the capital.
@if puddles>=3
MAMA DEE: Eddar! Trash. Alley. Now. Before the doors open and the whole Bay smells my kitchen.
@else
MAMA DEE: Eddar! Floor's still sticky! And then trash, alley, before the whole Bay smells my kitchen!
@endif
@end

@scene prologue.mama_dee
# CHARACTER: Mama Dee — owner, gravel voice, the only adult who looks out for Eddar.
@if p.amp_fixed & puddles>=3
@if seen.prologue.mama_dee>=2
MAMA DEE: Why are you still standing in front of me, sugar? Back door. Trash. Go be a legend of sanitation.
@end
@endif
MAMA DEE: Trash, sugar. Back door. And if Rex Valor's out there, you walk past him like he's weather.
EDDAR: Weather doesn't usually stuff me in a barrel.
MAMA DEE: Then you ain't seen weather in Solace Bay.
@end
@endif
@if p.amp_fixed
MAMA DEE: Heard the amp. Heard the punch, too. Don't tell Roxy I said this, but it's the best that thing's sounded since she bought it.
MAMA DEE: Floor's still sticky, though. Then trash.
@end
@endif
@if seen.prologue.mama_dee>=1
@if seen.prologue.mama_dee>=2
MAMA DEE: Honey, I love you like the son I had the good sense not to have. Go do something.
@end
@endif
MAMA DEE: Still here? You thinking about the Proclamation?
EDDAR: Thinking about how a scroll decides who's important.
MAMA DEE: Scroll never decided I was important. I bought a bar anyway. You want to matter, sugar, you don't wait on ink.
@end
@endif
MAMA DEE: You know what tonight is, Eddar? The Rubric reads out a name and some lucky fool becomes a legend.
EDDAR: Any chance it's the guy who mops?
MAMA DEE: Honey, prophecy don't mop. That's why it needs us.
@if puddles<3
MAMA DEE: Floor. Cola. Go.
@endif
@end

@scene prologue.spike
# CHARACTER: Spike — the bass player; deadpan; secretly fond of Eddar.
@if p.amp_fixed
@if talk.spike_after>=1
SPIKE: Still not telling her. My silence is a gift. Gifts can be withdrawn.
@end
@endif
SPIKE: I didn't tell her you punched the amp. I want that on the record. I'm holding it over you for life.
@set talk.spike_after += 1
@end
@endif
@if p.amp_hum_back
SPIKE: She's gonna burn the building down, man. I've seen it happen. Not this building. But a building.
@end
@endif
@if seen.prologue.spike>=1
@if seen.prologue.spike>=2
SPIKE: Three visits. We're basically married now. I get the jukebox in the divorce.
@end
@endif
SPIKE: You're back. Nobody comes back to talk to the bass player. Are you okay? Blink twice.
EDDAR: I just like you, Spike.
SPIKE: ...That's the weirdest thing anyone's said to me this year, and Lulu once told me she could taste colours.
@end
@endif
SPIKE: Bass player life: nobody hears you, everybody would notice if you stopped. Kind of like you, mop guy.
EDDAR: That's the nicest thing you've ever said to me.
SPIKE: Don't get used to it.
@end

@scene prologue.lulu
# CHARACTER: Lulu — the drummer; sunny chaos. PLOT: plants the Canticle's missing line (T6).
@if p.amp_fixed
@if talk.lulu_after>=1
LULU: Shh. I'm counting. One-two-three-four, one-two— you made me lose it. Worth it. Hi.
@end
@endif
LULU: Roxy's playing like the building owes her money. Good job, Eddar.
@set talk.lulu_after += 1
@end
@endif
@if seen.prologue.lulu>=1
@if seen.prologue.lulu>=2
LULU: If the Rubric picks a drummer, I'm going to cry. If they pick Rex, I'm also going to cry, but differently.
@end
@endif
LULU: Okay, I've been thinking about it. What's wrong with the Hero verse? You said it doesn't scan.
EDDAR: There's a beat missing. Every other verse in the Canticle runs eight lines. The Hero verse runs seven.
EDDAR: It reads like somebody cut a line out.
LULU: Ooh. Spooky. Drummers hate a missing beat. You can feel it in your teeth.
@end
@endif
LULU: I'm warming up. Don't talk to me. Actually, talk to me. Did you see the Proclamation scroll? They say the ink glows red when it picks the Hero.
EDDAR: I've read the Canticle. Twice. The Hero verse doesn't even scan.
LULU: You've READ it? The whole thing? On purpose?
EDDAR: I clean the Hall of Records at night. It was that or talk to the ghosts.
@end

@scene prologue.gil
# CHARACTER: barfly with micro-stories. PLOT: plants Sable's arrival.
@if seen.prologue.gil>=1
@if seen.prologue.gil>=2
GIL: I've told you everything I know, kid. Which, at my age, is mostly the names of horses that lost.
@end
@endif
GIL: Still thinking about that rider? Silver hair, black visor?
EDDAR: I didn't say anything.
GIL: You didn't have to. You've got the face of a boy who's been told a story with no ending.
GIL: Here's the ending, free of charge: she went up toward the Hall of Records. Where you work. Funny, that.
@end
@endif
GIL: Saw a woman ride in on a black light-cycle this afternoon. Visor. Silver hair. Didn't stop at the Proclamation tent.
GIL: Didn't stop anywhere. Just looked at every face on the boardwalk like she was reading a list.
EDDAR: Looking for someone?
GIL: Kid, everybody's looking for someone. Most of us are just looking for the bathroom.
@end

@scene prologue.jukebox
# CHARACTER: Eddar's quiet competence; Rex's ego (plants Rex's Anthem).
@if p.amp_fixed
EDDAR: Someone queued "Valor Forever (Demo)" four times. Rex has been here. Rex has been here, and Rex has recorded a demo.
@end
@endif
@if seen.prologue.jukebox>=1
EDDAR: B-side, slot 14: "Halcyon Days," 1962. Scratched on the chorus. I could fix it. I won't. Some songs should keep their scars.
@end
@endif
EDDAR: Mama Dee's jukebox. Every record is older than I am, except the one I fixed. I won't say which one. It knows.
@end

@scene prologue.front_door
# PLOT: Explains why the front is locked; points at the real exit.
EDDAR: Front door's locked until doors. Mama Dee's rule: nobody in till the floor's clean, and nobody out till the trash is.
EDDAR: The trash goes out the back. Stage right, past the amp, under the pink EXIT sign.
@end

@scene prologue.back_door_locked
# PLOT: Gate the alley until both chores are done; say which one is missing.
@if p.amp_fixed
MAMA DEE: Floor first, sugar! I can hear your shoes sticking from here!
@end
@endif
@if puddles>=3
EDDAR: The alley can wait. Roxy's amp can't. Soundcheck first, says the woman who signs my paychecks.
@end
@endif
EDDAR: The alley can wait. Floor first, then soundcheck, says the woman who signs my paychecks.
@end
